ence/"], "citations": [{"title": "HIPAA Security Rule To Strengthen the Cybersecurity of Electronic Protected Health Information (proposed rule)", "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/01/06/2024-30983/hipaa-security-rule-to-strengthen-the-cybersecurity-of-electronic-protected-health-information", "publisher": "Federal Register", "date": "6 Jan 2025"}, {"title": "HIPAA Security Rule NPRM fact sheet", "url": "https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/security/hipaa-security-rule-nprm/factsheet/index.html", "publisher": "US Department of Health and Human Services"}, {"title": "HIPAA Security Rule 2026: what to expect when OCR finalizes", "url": "https://medcurity.com/hipaa-security-rule-2026/", "publisher": "Medcurity"}, {"title": "HIPAA Security Rule proposed changes: status and what to do", "url": "https://www.beefedupit.com/blog/hipaa-security-rule-proposed-changes/", "publisher": "Beefed Up IT"}]}-->
<p>In January 2025 HHS published a proposed overhaul of the HIPAA Security Rule, the first major update in many years<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Healthcare organisations have been watching it since. As of this writing it still isn't final, and commentators report the expected timing has slipped, with regulatory agenda targets moving into 2027<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>.</p>
<p>My view as an architect working on health data: stop waiting for the final text. Most of the proposal describes controls a healthcare AI platform should have anyway. Confirm the legal detail with counsel; build the engineering now.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>What the proposal would change</h2>
<p>Based on HHS's own summary and the proposed rule, the headline changes include<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>:</p>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Proposed change</th><th>What it means for an AI platform</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Encryption</td><td>Required for ePHI at rest and in transit, with limited exceptions, instead of "addressable"</td><td>Every store holding ePHI is encrypted, including vector indexes, caches and logs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Multi-factor authentication</td><td>Required for access to systems with ePHI, with limited exceptions</td><td>MFA on consoles, notebooks, data tools and admin APIs, not just the main app</td></tr>
<tr><td>Asset inventory and network map</td><td>Written, maintained inventory of technology assets and data flows</td><td>You must know every place ePHI travels, including model providers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Testing and recovery</td><td>Regular vulnerability scanning, penetration testing and restoration expectations</td><td>Recovery is tested, not assumed</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Treat the specifics as proposed. The final rule could change, be scaled back or be withdrawn<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The AI-specific blind spots</h2>
<p>Traditional HIPAA programmes were built around EHRs and databases. AI systems add places for ePHI to hide that inventories often miss:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Embeddings and vector indexes</strong> built from clinical notes. They are derived from ePHI and should be protected like it.</li>
<li><strong>Prompt and response logs</strong>, which can contain full patient details if content capture is switched on. My <a href="/insights/llm-observability-opentelemetry-genai/">LLM observability</a> post covers capturing metadata without content.</li>
<li><strong>Evaluation datasets</strong> copied from production to test quality, often into less controlled environments.</li>
<li><strong>External model providers</strong>, which need appropriate agreements and a clear record of what data reaches them.</li>
<li><strong>Agent tool calls</strong> that move data between systems, which should be scoped and logged as in <a href="/insights/securing-mcp-servers-checklist/">securing MCP servers</a>.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><p>If your asset inventory doesn't list your vector database, your prompt logs and your model providers, it is incomplete, whatever the final rule says.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What I'd build now</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>A data-flow map for every AI use case</strong>: where ePHI enters, every store it lands in, and every service it reaches.</li>
<li><strong>Encryption everywhere by default</strong>, enforced in infrastructure as code so new stores can't be created unencrypted.</li>
<li><strong>MFA through a single identity provider</strong> for every tool that touches ePHI, including data science environments.</li>
<li><strong>An AI gateway</strong> that redacts or blocks ePHI before it reaches providers without the right agreements, and logs every call.</li>
<li><strong>Synthetic or de-identified data for evaluation</strong> wherever possible, with production samples handled under the same controls as production.</li>
<li><strong>Tested restores</strong> of the data platform and vector indexes on a schedule, with the results recorded.</li>
</ol>
<p>These sit naturally on top of a governed clinical data platform like the one in <a href="/insights/fhir-to-omop-architecture/">FHIR to OMOP</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The cost of waiting</h2>
<p>Commentary suggests organisations may get a limited window to comply once a final rule is published<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Retrofitting encryption, MFA and an accurate inventory across a sprawling AI estate in a few months is far harder than building them in as the estate grows. And the risk these controls reduce exists today, rule or no rule.</p>
</section>

